---
// Demo bench for `mountGlassLoupe` — the iOS "hold on a word" magnifier.
//
// Deliberately standalone (no DocsLayout, no Tailwind, no React island): the loupe
// clones live DOM and renders it in the top layer, so it's worth seeing the effect
// with nothing else in the stack that could be doing the work.
import '@liquidglassjs/core/css';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
    <title>Glass loupe · Liquid Glass</title>
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href={base + 'favicon.svg'} />
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <header class="head">
        <h1>Glass loupe</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Press and hold anywhere in the article, then drag. A glass capsule floats above the
          pointer, magnifying the line under it — the iOS text loupe, built from the same
          displacement lens as everything else in this library.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="controls" id="controls">
        <label>
          Trigger
          <select id="trigger">
            <option value="longpress">long press</option>
            <option value="press">press</option>
            <option value="hover">hover</option>
          </select>
        </label>
        <label>Zoom <input type="range" id="zoom" min="1.1" max="3" step="0.05" value="1.55" /></label>
        <label>Width <input type="range" id="width" min="60" max="280" step="2" value="132" /></label>
        <label>Height <input type="range" id="height" min="28" max="160" step="2" value="44" /></label>
        <label>Radius <input type="range" id="radius" min="0" max="80" step="1" value="22" /></label>
        <label>Strength <input type="range" id="strength" min="0" max="30" step="0.5" value="16" /></label>
        <label>Chroma <input type="range" id="chroma" min="0" max="1.5" step="0.02" value="0.6" /></label>
        <label>Dome <input type="range" id="dome" min="0" max="24" step="0.5" value="7" /></label>
        <label>Offset Y <input type="range" id="offsetY" min="-140" max="0" step="2" value="-54" /></label>
        <label>
          Hold <input type="range" id="longPressMs" min="0" max="900" step="20" value="400" />
          <span id="holdVal">400ms</span>
        </label>
        <label class="check">
          <input type="checkbox" id="snap" checked /> snap to line
        </label>
        <output id="caret">—</output>
      </div>

      <article id="article" class="article">
        <h2>Building glass for the web</h2>
        <p>
          The trick that makes any of this work is that a displacement map is just an image. Encode
          a height field into a bitmap — red for how far each pixel should slide sideways, green for
          how far it should slide down — and <code>feDisplacementMap</code> will push the pixels of a
          live DOM subtree around accordingly. No canvas, no WebGL, no snapshot: the text underneath
          stays real text, selectable and searchable and crisp at any zoom.
        </p>
        <p>
          A lens is that same map, sized to a small rounded rectangle and dropped into the filter
          through a positioned <code>feImage</code>. Moving the lens is a matter of moving that one
          node; the map itself never gets rebuilt, which is why dragging holds its frame rate even on
          a phone. Everything outside the lens region encodes to neutral grey — no displacement at
          all — so the rest of the page passes through untouched.
        </p>
        <p>
          Magnification, though, is a different problem. A displacement map bends pixels; it cannot
          scale them. To actually enlarge what is underneath, something has to render the content a
          second time at a larger size — and if you want the result to stay sharp, that something had
          better not be a bitmap.
        </p>
        <p>
          Hence the clone. The magnifier deep-copies the source subtree, scales it with a CSS
          transform, and clips it to the capsule. Because it is still DOM, the glyphs are rasterised
          at their final size and land pin-sharp. The lens filter then sits on top of that copy, so
          the rim refracts the magnified text exactly the way it refracts everything else.
        </p>
      </article>

      <h2 class="sub">Same thing, as a custom element</h2>
      <p class="note">
        The markup below is a <code>&lt;glass-loupe&gt;</code> with no JavaScript of its own — the
        consumption path for Vue, Svelte, plain HTML, or Astro. Hover it.
      </p>
      <glass-loupe id="ce" trigger="hover" zoom="1.9" width="150" height="50" radius="25">
        <blockquote>
          A lens is a small image and a positioned <code>feImage</code>; a magnifier is that lens
          over a second copy of the page. Neither one needs a canvas.
        </blockquote>
      </glass-loupe>

      <p class="note">
        Selecting text still works: hold <em>still</em> for the hold duration and you get the loupe;
        press and <em>drag</em> and you get an ordinary selection. Only once the hold has won does
        the loupe take <code>user-select</code> away, and it hands it back on release — which is also
        what keeps iOS Safari from stacking its native loupe on top of this one.
      </p>
    </main>

    <script>
      import '@liquidglassjs/element/loupe'; // registers <glass-loupe>; no wiring needed
      import { mountGlassLoupe } from '@liquidglassjs/core';
      import type { GlassLoupe, GlassLoupeTrigger } from '@liquidglassjs/core';

      const article = document.getElementById('article') as HTMLElement;
      const caretOut = document.getElementById('caret') as HTMLOutputElement;
      const $ = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id) as HTMLInputElement;

      const PARAMS = [
        'zoom',
        'longPressMs',
        'width',
        'height',
        'radius',
        'strength',
        'chroma',
        'dome',
        'offsetY',
      ] as const;

      let loupe: GlassLoupe | null = null;

      const readParams = () =>
        Object.fromEntries(PARAMS.map((k) => [k, Number($(k).value)])) as Record<string, number>;

      const syncHold = () => {
        document.getElementById('holdVal')!.textContent = $('longPressMs').value + 'ms';
      };

      // Only trigger and snapToLine are mount-time options, so changing either remounts.
      // Everything else — the hold duration included — tunes live via reconfigure().
      const mount = () => {
        loupe?.dispose();
        syncHold();
        loupe = mountGlassLoupe({
          source: article,
          trigger: ($('trigger') as unknown as HTMLSelectElement).value as GlassLoupeTrigger,
          snapToLine: $('snap').checked,
          ...readParams(),
          onMove: (s) => {
            caretOut.textContent = s.caret
              ? `“${(s.caret.node.nodeValue ?? '').slice(Math.max(0, s.caret.offset - 12), s.caret.offset + 12).trim()}”`
              : '—';
          },
          onHide: () => {
            caretOut.textContent = '—';
          },
        });
      };

      mount();
      $('trigger').addEventListener('change', mount);
      $('snap').addEventListener('change', mount);
      PARAMS.forEach((k) =>
        $(k).addEventListener('input', () => {
          loupe?.reconfigure(readParams());
          syncHold();
        }),
      );

      // Handy for poking at it from the console / automation.
      (window as unknown as { loupe: () => GlassLoupe | null }).loupe = () => loupe;
    </script>

    <style>
      :root {
        --paper: #fbfaf7;
        --ink: #16151a;
        --muted: #6c6a75;
        color-scheme: light dark;
      }
      @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
        :root {
          --paper: #131218;
          --ink: #ece9f2;
          --muted: #9b98a6;
        }
      }
      body {
        margin: 0;
        background: var(--paper);
        color: var(--ink);
        font: 16px/1.6 ui-sans-serif, system-ui, -apple-system, 'Segoe UI', sans-serif;
      }
      main {
        max-width: 46rem;
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 3rem 1.5rem 6rem;
      }
      h1 {
        font-size: 2rem;
        margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
        letter-spacing: -0.02em;
      }
      .lede {
        color: var(--muted);
        margin: 0 0 2rem;
      }
      .controls {
        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(11rem, 1fr));
        gap: 0.6rem 1.2rem;
        padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
        margin-bottom: 2rem;
        border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 12%, transparent);
        border-radius: 14px;
        font-size: 0.8rem;
        color: var(--muted);
      }
      .controls label {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.5rem;
        justify-content: space-between;
      }
      .controls label.check {
        justify-content: flex-start;
      }
      .controls input[type='range'] {
        flex: 1;
        min-width: 0;
      }
      #caret {
        grid-column: 1 / -1;
        font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace;
        color: var(--ink);
      }
      .article {
        /* Deliberately no background of its own — the loupe should find the page's
           and fill the capsule with it, rather than showing a see-through hole. */
        border-radius: 16px;
      }
      .article h2 {
        font-size: 1.25rem;
        letter-spacing: -0.01em;
        margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
      }
      .article p {
        margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
        text-wrap: pretty;
      }
      .article code {
        font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace;
        font-size: 0.9em;
        padding: 0.1em 0.3em;
        border-radius: 5px;
        background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 8%, transparent);
      }
      .sub {
        font-size: 1.1rem;
        margin: 3rem 0 0.5rem;
        border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 12%, transparent);
        padding-top: 2rem;
      }
      .note {
        color: var(--muted);
        font-size: 0.85rem;
      }
      blockquote {
        margin: 1rem 0 0;
        padding: 1.1rem 1.3rem;
        border-left: 3px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink) 25%, transparent);
        font-size: 1.05rem;
      }
    </style>
  </body>
</html>
